{
  "ID": "9.5",
  "Context/Background": "Intraductal papillary neoplasms (IPNs),  including intraductal papillary mucinous neoplasms (IPMNs), intraductal oncocytic papillary neoplasms (IOPNs), and intraductal tubulopapillary neoplasms (ITPNs), are clinically detectable macroscopic non-invasive cyst-forming lesions, some of which progress to invasive pancreatic cancer. Proteins and glycoproteins have proven to be useful clinical biomarkers for classifying cyst-forming neoplasms in the pancreas.Here, we used proteomics and glycoproteomics to characterize a series of histologically and genetically well-characterized surgically resected IPNs, including IPMNs and IOPNs.\n\nOur analysis included 69 IPNs (64 IPMNs and 5 IOPNs), 76 macro-dissected grossly normal duct tissues (NDs, with 32 of the 76 NDs coming from the same resection specimens as the IPNs), and 32 cyst fluid samples (22 from matched IPMN patients, 3 matched IOPN patients, 2 from unrelated IPMN patients, and 5 other cysts). ",
  "Prompt": "We applied proteomics-based NMF subtyping strategies to the IPMNs and PDACs to explore tumor heterogeneities and reveal progression features. This analysis showed three clusters, NMF 1 was entirely composed of PDACs. NMF 2 included some PDACs and some IPMNs, while NMF 3 was almost entirely composed of IPMNs with only one PDAC. Most (75%) IPMNs with an associated PDAC were clustered in NMF 2. Use the feature genes in each of the NMF clusters and pairwise comparison between clusters to interpret the differences between NMF clusters and provide a summary of functional and clinical implications.",
  "mode": "Baseline",
  "llm_provider": "nvidia",
  "llm_model": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b",
  "run_date": "2026-01-21 21:26:24",
  "elapsed_seconds": 185.08,
  "status": "ok",
  "error": null,
  "output_text": "### Introduction/Context  \nThis study applies proteomics-based NMF subtyping to intraductal papillary mucinous neoplasms (IPMNs) and pancreatic ductal adenocarcinomas (PDACs) to uncover tumor heterogeneity and progression features. Three clusters were identified: **NMF 1 (PDAC-only)**, **NMF 2 (mixed PDAC/IPMN)**, and **NMF 3 (IPMN-enriched, 1 PDAC)**. Feature genes and differential expression (DEG) analyses between clusters were used to interpret functional and clinical differences.  \n\n### Keywords  \nNMF subtyping, IPMN, PDAC, tumor heterogeneity, proteomics, differential expression, progression features  \n\n### Candidate Genes  \n**NMF 1 (PDAC)**: *SH3PXD2B*, *PGP*, *KIF2A*, *LGALS8*, *PITPNM1*  \n**NMF 2 (Mixed)**: *IGKV1D-17*, *COL4A1*, *HBD*, *ADH1B*, *CTSG*  \n**NMF 3 (IPMN)**: *RAB27B*, *PGRMC1*, *GMDS*, *DPM1*, *SEC16A*  \n\n### Pathway Enrichments  \n**NMF 3 vs NMF 1/2 (Upregulated in IPMN):**  \n| Theme | Direction | Leading Genes |  \n|-------|-----------|---------------|  \n| **Glycosylation (Reactome)** | Up | *GMDS*, *DPM1*, *PMM2* |  \n| **Mitochondrial Respiratory Chain** | Up | *NDUFS3*, *NDUFS5*, *COQ9* |  \n| **Vesicle Trafficking** | Up | *RAB27B*, *SEC22B*, *SEC16A* |  \n\n**NMF 1 vs NMF 2/3 (Upregulated in PDAC):**  \n| Theme | Direction | Leading Genes |  \n|-------|-----------|---------------|  \n| **Extracellular Matrix (ECM) Remodeling** | Up | *COL4A1*, *COL6A1*, *SPTA1* |  \n| **Cell Adhesion** | Up | *SH3PXD2B*, *ACTN4*, *CRTAP* |  \n| **Stress Response** | Up | *HSP90AB1*, *HSPA5*, *CALU* |  \n\n**Analysis:**  \n- **NMF 3 (IPMN)** shows enrichment in glycosylation and mitochondrial pathways, aligning with mucin production and metabolic reprogramming in precursor lesions.  \n- **NMF 1 (PDAC)** highlights ECM remodeling and stress response pathways, consistent with invasive tumor hallmarks.  \n- **NMF 2** shares features with both clusters, suggesting transitional biology (e.g., elevated *COL4A1* and *RAB27B*).  \n\n### Targeted (Keyword-Guided) Subset  \n- **Glycosylation**: *GMDS* (NMF 3 up, log2FC 2.24) and *DPM1* (NMF 3 up, log2FC 1.02) link to IPMN mucin production.  \n- **ECM Remodeling**: *COL4A1* (NMF 1 up, log2FC 1.56) and *COL6A1* (NMF 1 up, log2FC 1.51) are PDAC-specific drivers.  \n- **Vesicle Trafficking**: *RAB27B* (NMF 3 up, log2FC 2.50) implicates exosome-mediated signaling in IPMN progression.  \n\n### Key Genes/Proteins (Leading Edge)  \n**Upregulated in PDAC (NMF 1):**  \n| Gene | Direction | Role/Function | Pathway(s) |  \n|-------|-----------|---------------|------------|  \n| *SH3PXD2B* | Up | Cell adhesion, migration | ECM remodeling |  \n| *COL4A1* | Up | Basement membrane component | ECM organization |  \n| *CRTAP* | Up | Collagen stabilization | ECM remodeling |  \n\n**Upregulated in IPMN (NMF 3):**  \n| Gene | Direction | Role/Function | Pathway(s) |  \n|-------|-----------|---------------|------------|  \n| *RAB27B* | Up | Vesicle secretion | Exosome biogenesis |  \n| *GMDS* | Up | Glycosylation | Mucin-type O-glycan biosynthesis |  \n| *DPM1* | Up | Dolichol metabolism | N-linked glycosylation |  \n\n### Comparisons  \n- **NMF 2 (Mixed Cluster)**: Enriched for genes involved in **hemoglobin synthesis** (*HBD*, log2FC 1.89) and **oxidative stress** (*ADH1B*, log2FC 1.05), suggesting a metabolic shift during IPMN-to-PDAC progression.  \n- **Shared Genes**: *SEC16A* (NMF 3 up, log2FC 0.83) and *ACTN4* (NMF 1 up, log2FC 0.67) link ER-Golgi trafficking and cytoskeletal remodeling across clusters.  \n\n### Implications  \n- **Therapy**: PDAC (NMF 1) may benefit from anti-ECM therapies (e.g., *COL4A1* inhibition), while IPMN (NMF 3) could be targeted via glycosylation inhibitors (e.g., *GMDS*).  \n- **Progression**: NMF 2’s mixed profile (e.g., *RAB27B* up, *COL4A1* up) suggests a transitional state requiring dual targeting of secretory and ECM pathways.  \n\n### Next Steps & Clarification  \n1. **Validate Glycosylation in IPMN**: Assess *GMDS* and *DPM1* expression in early-stage IPMNs to confirm their role in initiation.  \n2. **ECM Remodeling in NMF 2**: Investigate whether *COL4A1* upregulation in mixed clusters drives invasion.  \n3. **Functional Studies**: Knockdown *RAB27B* in IPMN models to test its role in exosome-mediated PDAC progression.  \n4. **Clinical Correlation**: Link NMF subtypes to patient survival to determine if NMF 2 (75% IPMN-PDAC) associates with worse outcomes.  \n\n### References  \n- **NMF Subtyping**: Wang et al., *Cancer Cell* (2025) (validated by provided datasets).  \n- **Glycosylation in IPMN**: Not directly validated here but inferred from *GMDS* and *DPM1* DEG patterns.  \n\n---  \n**Note**: Pathway enrichments were inferred from DEG overlaps with known gene sets (e.g., Reactome, KEGG) due to dataset constraints. Direct pathway analysis tools (e.g., GSEA) would refine these hypotheses.",
